  "body": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "This recipe serves 4 and takes about 45 minutes."
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Sukiyaki"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "is a celebratory Japanese hot pot. Thin slices of beef cook in a bubbling"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "warishita"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "sauce alongside"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "tofu-firm"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ", spring onions and noodles. Diners dip each piece in beaten raw egg before eating, a tradition that softens the strong seasoning of the broth."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Make the warishita in advance so the sugar dissolves fully before the pan goes on. The ratio is soy, mirin, sake and sugar. It should taste intensely salty-sweet on its own because it dilutes as the ingredients release moisture into the pan. Add the beef in small amounts at a time. It only needs 1 to 2 minutes in the broth. Remove it quickly and let diners eat as they go rather than cooking everything at once."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
