      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the warishita. Combine the soy sauce, mirin, sake and sugar in a jug. Stir until the sugar dissolves. Taste. It should be intensely sweet and salty. Set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Arrange all the ingredients on plates in the centre of the table: the raw beef, tofu, noodles and spring onions. Beat one raw egg into each individual dipping bowl and set one at each place."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Place a cast-iron pan over a medium heat on the hob, or on a portable burner at the table. Add the vegetable oil and let it heat."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add a few slices of beef in a single layer. Pour over about a third of the warishita. The broth will sizzle. Turn the beef after 1 minute. Remove and eat dipped in the raw egg."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the tofu, spring onions and noodles to the pan between rounds of beef. Pour in more warishita as the pan dries out. Add a splash of dashi if the sauce becomes too thick or salty."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Continue adding ingredients in batches, eating as you go. Serve with bowls of steamed rice."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "The broth has become too salty partway through the meal",
              "cause": "The meat and vegetables released moisture but not enough to dilute the warishita",
              "fix": "Add dashi stock or water a splash at a time. Taste before adding more warishita."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "The tofu is breaking apart in the pan",
              "cause": "Tofu was too soft or was stirred too aggressively",
              "fix": "Use firm tofu only and add it gently. Move it as little as possible once it is in the broth."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Diners are concerned about eating raw egg",
              "cause": "Unfamiliarity with the Japanese sukiyaki tradition",
              "fix": "Use eggs that are pasteurised or clearly marked as safe to eat raw. The egg acts as a cooling agent and rounds out the rich sauce. Diners can skip the egg and eat the beef directly."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Sukiyaki became popular in Japan during the Meiji era when beef eating, long associated with Buddhism-influenced food taboos, was officially embraced as a sign of modernisation. The dish takes its name from the sukiyaki pot, a shallow iron pan originally used to cook over an open fire. Today it is a winter dish shared at the table, often marking special occasions. Families have their own warishita ratios passed down through generations. Tokyo and Kansai styles differ slightly in the ratio of sweet to salty, but the communal, cook-at-the-table nature of the dish is constant across Japan."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
